{#
  Shared <mj-head>: THE breakpoint, meta, brand defaults, styles, responsive rules.
  Colour scheme is pinned light for every email — see the LIGHT ONLY note below.
  Colours/fonts come from `tokens`.
#}

{#
  ── THE BREAKPOINT ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  ONE number for the whole system, declared once, here. Email has exactly two states — desktop
  multi-column and mobile stack — so there is no sm/md/lg/xl scale and no second mock. Do not add
  another media-query width anywhere: build/validate-email.ts fails the build if a third breakpoint
  appears in the output.

  This is what makes every mj-column stack: MJML compiles it into `@media (min-width:480px)` rules
  that widen the columns above the breakpoint. The `max-width:479px` block at the bottom of this
  file is the OTHER HALF of the same breakpoint (480 − 1) — the two are checked against
  config.ts BREAKPOINT_PX so they can never drift apart.

  Note what this does NOT do: clients that strip <style> (New Outlook, Gmail on a non-Google
  account) never see that media query, so the breakpoint alone would leave them stacked even on
  desktop. That is what `css-class="fluid"` is for — see applyFluidMaxWidth.
#}
<mj-breakpoint width="480px" />

{#
  ── LIGHT ONLY ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  These emails are light-only by decision, not by default: pinning the scheme tells dark-mode
  clients to render the brand as designed instead of auto-inverting it. (The GALLERY has a dark
  theme — that is the tooling UI, a real web page, and has nothing to do with the emails.)

  There is deliberately no `meta.colorScheme:'auto'` branch any more. It was an unused option
  waiting on a dark-safe logo that does not exist, i.e. exactly the speculative config
  docs/architecture.md warns about. If a dark treatment is ever wanted, it comes back as a real
  feature (dark-safe logo + themed surfaces), not as a flag.

  HONEST LIMIT: this pin is respected by Apple Mail, iOS Mail and Outlook.com. The Gmail app on
  Android force-inverts light backgrounds regardless of any meta — nothing in the HTML can prevent
  that, which is why the logo being a dark wordmark on transparent still matters there. Tracked in
  docs/tokens.md.
#}
<mj-raw>
  <meta name="color-scheme" content="light">
  <meta name="supported-color-schemes" content="light">
</mj-raw>

{# Apple Mail / iOS "data detectors" auto-link anything that looks like a phone number, date or
   postal address and restyle it as blue underlined text — which lands squarely on the two office
   addresses in blocks/company-legal.njk and turns them into map links in the brand's own footer.
   Turning detection off is invisible in every other client. The WhatsApp phone number stays
   tappable because it is a real <a href="https://wa.me/…">, not a detected one. #}
<mj-raw>
  <meta name="format-detection" content="telephone=no,date=no,address=no,email=no">
</mj-raw>

{# Load the brand web font for clients that support it (Apple Mail, iOS…). Outlook/Gmail
   ignore it and fall back to the native system stack in tokens.font.stack. #}
<mj-font name="Inter" href="{$ tokens.font.webFontUrl $}" />

<mj-attributes>
  <mj-all font-family="{$ tokens.font.stack $}" />
  {# 15px body: ~71 characters per line in the 536px column — inside the comfortable 50-75 band,
     and it leaves the 16px headings/labels a step above it so the scale is not flat. #}
  <mj-text font-size="15px" line-height="1.6" color="{$ tokens.color.body $}" padding="0" />
  <mj-section background-color="{$ tokens.color.white $}" padding="0" />
  <mj-button font-weight="700" font-size="15px" inner-padding="12px 30px" border-radius="{$ tokens.radius.lg $}" />
</mj-attributes>

<mj-style inline="inline">
  .link-orange a { color: {$ tokens.color.orangeStrong $} !important; text-decoration: none; }
  .brand { color: {$ tokens.color.orangeStrong $}; font-weight: 700; }
</mj-style>

<mj-style>
  /* Contact links (WhatsApp + email): subtle by default, orange + underline on hover.
     Hover only fires on clients with embedded CSS + a mouse (desktop webmail, Apple Mail). */
  .contact-link a:hover { color: {$ tokens.color.orangeStrong $} !important; text-decoration: underline !important; }
  /* comacpro.net link: orange always, underline only on hover */
  .link-orange a:hover { text-decoration: underline !important; }

  /* Avatar: crop-to-fill (no distortion) for a non-square source. object-fit works in Apple Mail /
     iOS / most webmail; Outlook ignores it, so the hosted avatar should still be a square crop. */
  .avatar-img img { object-fit: cover !important; object-position: center !important; }

  /* Reserve heights so feature titles / equipment labels align across the grid, even when one
     wraps to a second line — without touching the copy.

     The ` div` is REQUIRED and was the bug: MJML puts an mj-text's css-class on the wrapping <td>,
     and `min-height` does not apply to table cells (CSS 2.1 §10.7). So `.feat-title {min-height}`
     was silently dead — a one-line title left its description 20px higher than a two-line one,
     which is exactly what this rule exists to prevent. mj-text renders <td><div>…</div></td>, so
     the reservation has to sit on the div. (Same pattern as `.stat-num div` in buyer-invitation.)

     Embedded CSS: the style-stripping clients lose it and simply align to content. Cosmetic only. */
  .feat-title div { min-height: 44px; }
  .feat-desc div { min-height: 104px; }
  .eq-label div { min-height: 34px; }

  /* ── MOBILE — the one and only breakpoint (479 = BREAKPOINT_PX − 1) ──────────────────────────
     Everything below the breakpoint lives in THIS block. Adding a second width here is a build
     error (assertSingleBreakpoint), so keep mobile a single state.

     Reminder on reach: this is embedded CSS. Outlook DESKTOP (Word engine) renders at full width
     and never reads it — which is fine, because desktop is what these rules undo. New Outlook and
     Gmail-GANGA also drop it; there the inline `max-width` from `.fluid` does the reflowing, so
     nothing here is load-bearing for layout — only for polish (gutters, type sizes). */
  @media only screen and (max-width:479px) {
    /* Fluid-hybrid columns: drop the injected max-width cap so stacked columns go full-width.
       One rule for every `fluid` column — a new fluid column is covered automatically.
       (mj-group columns carry an inline width:% and never stack; they are not `fluid`.)

       ⚠ THE TRAP, if you write an n-across override below: this sets `display:block`, and a
       50%-wide BLOCK still stacks vertically — you get one column crammed into half the screen.
       Any rule that narrows a `fluid` column to sit N-across MUST also restore
       `display:inline-block !important`. That is what the .eq-col/.feat-col rule does. */
    .fluid { display: block !important; width: 100% !important; max-width: none !important; }
    /* Paired CTAs: gap between the stacked buttons; MJML puts the side gutters on the inner td. */
    .cta-col { padding: 0 0 12px 0 !important; }
    .cta-col td { padding-left: 0 !important; padding-right: 0 !important; }
    /* Equipment 6-across and features 4-across both collapse to 2-across — NOT to one column:
       stacking six equipment cards would add ~650px of scroll for six words each.
       `display:inline-block` is load-bearing here, not decoration: it undoes the `display:block`
       from .fluid above, which would otherwise make these 50% boxes stack in the left half. */
    .eq-col, .feat-col { display: inline-block !important; width: 50% !important; max-width: 50% !important; }
    /* Drop ALL the desktop height reservations here. At 2-across (~180px) every title, label and
       description fits without the wrap the reservations exist to absorb, so keeping them would add
       pure empty space — ~100px of it down the email — for an alignment problem mobile doesn't have. */
    .feat-title div, .feat-desc div, .eq-label div { min-height: 0 !important; }
    /* Stacked offices: gap between them + drop the desktop side gutter so both sit flush-left. */
    .office-col { padding: 0 0 18px 0 !important; }
    .office-col td { padding-left: 0 !important; padding-right: 0 !important; }
    /* Callout: stack the icon above the text (centered) instead of side-by-side */
    .co-icon, .co-text { display: block !important; text-align: center !important; }
    .co-icon { padding: 0 0 8px 0 !important; }
    .co-text { padding: 0 12px !important; }
    /* Signature on mobile: avatar left-aligned (same axis as the details), then details below. */
    .sig-avatar { padding: 0 0 14px 0 !important; }
  }
</mj-style>
